THE TWO HEADER BUTTONS I MISSED.

He spotted it: his panel has a "-" and a magenta "X" hard against the top right,
and my rebuild had neither. Picture 1 shows "-" beside the X; picture 2 shows the
SAME panel with "+" in that slot - so minus collapses to the title bar and plus
restores it. One state toggle, not two controls.

OBJ_BUTTON, not OBJ_LABEL. A label raises no click event: it would have looked
exactly like his button and done nothing - the decoration failure this repo keeps
rediscovering. The handler clears OBJPROP_STATE every time, because MT5 latches a
button down and a latched button reads as "no change" on the next click and
appears dead.

  "-" / "+"  collapse to the 40px title bar, and restore. Collapse DELETES the
             body objects rather than hiding them: an OBJ_LABEL behind a
             rectangle still renders on top of price.
  "X"        closes the panel completely, as his does. Every object is removed so
             a closed panel cannot leave a stale verdict on the chart looking
             current, and one line goes to the Experts log saying how to get it
             back - the panel is gone, so it cannot say so on the chart itself.

Both are runtime state, deliberately NOT inputs: a collapsed panel surviving a
terminal restart is a setting nobody remembers changing.

Session/Spread moved x+1290 -> x+1180. At the new 1500 width the old position ran
straight through where the buttons now sit.

The feed, the buffers and the alerts keep running while the panel is collapsed or
closed. The panel is a view, and closing a view must not stop the evidence.

